#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "87580982-1d5a-5f68-b9c8-df77b4da0196" :question/math-academy-id "e-5439" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124244 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f3694e9e-804f-55f0-9fb1-a78caec29224" :question/math-academy-id "e-5440" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124245 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "fc0725a9-354c-5e01-a924-12c0d2723ba3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5441" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124273 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5ba5ded2-bfca-5e53-9056-8aede726505f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5616" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fc770bf-0581-5b4b-849f-9ed418837d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370391} {:answer/id #uuid "60c85d54-c640-5c24-b63a-5693d5112c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370392} {:answer/id #uuid "f8886185-ef1f-5f17-8bdc-4d2143b80373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370393} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e6228f-1e47-5ff6-82ff-83097aae3f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370394} {:answer/id #uuid "57e003d1-c268-5a77-9265-f64e72016ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57e003d1-c268-5a77-9265-f64e72016ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39085a9-b936-5738-be42-340684e52456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a488962-0aee-5f33-834e-77bf191c7367" :question/math-academy-id "q-139998" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y \\\\ 6x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is ${v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad04e7cc-d439-5a9b-9ff7-e347e2befbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370398} {:answer/id #uuid "a11586be-b868-58ca-967c-bc8ab3b918fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370399} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc03676-6484-5064-b8c6-30bd6a5225e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370400} {:answer/id #uuid "c209b194-5b3f-5fe7-b0db-90209be27d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370401} {:answer/id #uuid "b3539cb7-00b3-52ba-8854-5cbcb5bceb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3539cb7-00b3-52ba-8854-5cbcb5bceb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370402} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97a0fb8-fb6c-5c31-b4c3-51083350d332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecf2c30f-d27f-5d2d-8234-9467ffd2f974" :question/math-academy-id "q-140011" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y \\\\ 5x+10y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot k+10\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2k+12 \\\\ 5k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k+12=0 \\\\ 5k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5f638d2-230e-5649-9702-4ba37dd22f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370405} {:answer/id #uuid "0785d710-b055-5734-9681-fa358e674c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370406} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9de56a-d495-5846-bdf6-e6e57dfa91d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370407} {:answer/id #uuid "d94445c2-7f2d-5fe4-af7e-e758cc90969c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370408} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c259ae-09be-5b29-8e16-8d27a64b8aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9de56a-d495-5846-bdf6-e6e57dfa91d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370407} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50f12b1-8336-5902-96b3-28585748060a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0f0ace1-f32a-5a2a-aecf-7a206aa0a8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140052" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -6x+3y\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{6}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=y[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5827b2ac-63e3-5619-92ee-286ec58822b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370412} {:answer/id #uuid "78eb1717-7b77-5cd3-b5ba-dccdb0829c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370413} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc6aba3-7567-5c52-ac36-82417d1bc4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370414} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1c4602-e00f-57ae-ac74-04e1b4c58934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370415} {:answer/id #uuid "30423ba0-a9f1-56de-bdde-3d5a003d2c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30423ba0-a9f1-56de-bdde-3d5a003d2c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370416} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41c3533-d3d0-54c4-8002-a96b5112ac4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d790d268-b73d-5bb7-b6c6-b72646e9b5be" :question/math-academy-id "q-37712" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{aligned}]$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e495a5f6-0248-5a58-b5a8-ffa5ab5c9793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123143} {:answer/id #uuid "009e5efc-95fb-5966-95f6-d44fe76e54a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123144} {:answer/id #uuid "5522728d-9cb4-5dcc-8ec5-dd62426cd2dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123145} {:answer/id #uuid "76532239-cf78-5418-947a-b1b521b5e8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123146} {:answer/id #uuid "781ddf16-86f5-53be-a452-ad286ac28341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009e5efc-95fb-5966-95f6-d44fe76e54a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123144} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e10e1a3-7666-53e1-9810-7b16f963ddc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c08047d4-78e2-5052-a86f-f3f6f58c0260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37825" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -10x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ -10\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4k-12 \\\\ -10k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4k-12=0 \\\\ -10k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fd26c3d-f29f-5214-b731-5e5d8e0de1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370419} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c70a6c-aa06-5505-bb49-ae51629b519d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370420} {:answer/id #uuid "406349f8-4225-5116-b8a8-17a591fc66b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370421} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6986a-9d71-5a28-beaf-84c8de102059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370422} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e7abf-1e33-5f75-8e62-99bda363d6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6986a-9d71-5a28-beaf-84c8de102059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370422} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea809e15-d057-5f6c-80c8-08af76a83790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16af1d3a-241b-5541-b15e-81549dff6d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-37827" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7228404a-bc96-527b-9b09-86de3fcb8187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370426} {:answer/id #uuid "0a478823-f92d-5b58-85d0-8173776be37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370427} {:answer/id #uuid "0183b795-a839-508b-b621-24fb98fb889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370428} {:answer/id #uuid "860ec080-cf32-53d9-bd83-25879e914bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370429} {:answer/id #uuid "9422f8a8-298a-51a2-af93-86f2a70b237d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7228404a-bc96-527b-9b09-86de3fcb8187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370426} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1dc666-15ab-5a30-bbcb-a11ba3f1e0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34778fc0-93c4-5c13-b7d6-916e1d349a22" :question/math-academy-id "q-37828" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d55380e4-27b7-50d5-96d0-2125f277ea66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370433} {:answer/id #uuid "65c3d146-9de3-50b0-a2f6-6dcf539fcc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370434} {:answer/id #uuid "02c54495-79a3-51eb-8080-31ef54ddad19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370435} {:answer/id #uuid "9abff7cb-79c9-5372-8753-89054059e254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370436} {:answer/id #uuid "8354d683-1ece-5770-8f93-3ab4845a981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c3d146-9de3-50b0-a2f6-6dcf539fcc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370434} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b609be-298c-5d4d-a82f-4c4fa38e0755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c58fed1-6a3f-5273-bb50-b5deba510447" :question/math-academy-id "q-37840" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+4z \\\\ -5x+10y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c37d69b7-b064-5cea-b2b2-463ae85c5522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123161} {:answer/id #uuid "ea29b905-c61f-53b2-a991-de3ce60ff466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123162} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe11a09-87e1-5271-a62e-6e583d413453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123163} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5eb3ac-40e4-5b06-af7d-9b417ba02ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123164} {:answer/id #uuid "703a5840-d825-52f0-ab9f-54e00027d6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe11a09-87e1-5271-a62e-6e583d413453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123163} :answer-field/id #uuid "4352bdde-5de2-5734-846c-2ad582ec5cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9bef4f8-9900-515d-bbba-da49134e795e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37842" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-8x+4y \\\\ 6x-3y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot k \\\\ 6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot 3-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-24+4k \\\\ 18-3k \\\\ 6-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-24+4k=0 \\\\ 18-3k=0 \\\\ 6-k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6aa692-e632-521d-bb73-83ccdec07908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123329} {:answer/id #uuid "931e1a1a-a795-5470-a1f7-e580ad989166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123330} {:answer/id #uuid "f101e727-977c-5b91-b3d3-271080939dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123331} {:answer/id #uuid "078d865f-d69d-5791-b421-8b94a1044b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123332} {:answer/id #uuid "6fee30fa-249e-5a0c-82f5-6f51c2eaafc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c6aa692-e632-521d-bb73-83ccdec07908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123329} :answer-field/id #uuid "4776b888-ff4b-5704-8363-e0555bd6438e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61ec1738-c727-5f71-a158-a3fc80b2a14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-38676" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-7y \\\\ 14y-10x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1=3\\cdot 1+1=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc5d5c4-9f8d-5f55-a6f3-671c7cc8f93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370439} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec5b23f-98d8-5922-987f-5c541ae97701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370440} {:answer/id #uuid "19ddfbac-b416-5338-89fb-4b3fabad153e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370441} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca56609-b7ea-53d4-8820-0607311a31c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370442} {:answer/id #uuid "1e70861a-4ab3-5b24-9756-602cb23ed696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e70861a-4ab3-5b24-9756-602cb23ed696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370443} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b803b6c-83a0-5ac0-9349-4eb571ebadd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70fb9633-c0bc-5291-ae81-e236155dd61d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38719" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ From the third equation, we obtain $z=0$ and from the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a31c458-a83e-5857-947f-c95f0fc6bc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370446} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcb9938-d9b7-58da-adb1-ab15e25eaf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370447} {:answer/id #uuid "c93018e8-2e15-57ba-9281-d4c850250bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370448} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ff6a0f-308e-51f6-8b78-49c8d4d3caba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370449} {:answer/id #uuid "fbae4e9c-55e6-52ba-9679-c78aee49bb46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a31c458-a83e-5857-947f-c95f0fc6bc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370446} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad01c371-8dc5-5e30-933f-bf89de213b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daa0bc39-0770-52e4-a043-5e620f02bd66" :question/math-academy-id "q-40175" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfee4b62-4bb8-5a15-81cd-f73597143032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370453} {:answer/id #uuid "2f403c5e-908a-596d-9f67-ee9f51dfc29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370454} {:answer/id #uuid "47879b2a-1019-53dd-b7c5-9b0909f0323a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370455} {:answer/id #uuid "925e75f7-1cdf-5355-9487-ba0f0675662f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370456} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c6893e-4531-54a0-9fc6-b8ac2a6542ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47879b2a-1019-53dd-b7c5-9b0909f0323a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370455} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e944f9a-36eb-52f9-a6e4-6e350e3163ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "554c3563-4c0d-5d18-8e54-b01c022c59b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40177" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}],$ find $9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4=9\\cdot 0+4=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477dd1da-92b7-52aa-a991-99c846c425f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370460} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6c5697-ca57-505d-9bb5-5aacb40c6348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370461} {:answer/id #uuid "1977e053-5aab-540e-b2fa-f0035e6d4ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370462} {:answer/id #uuid "293f1015-3b1f-50ee-8160-7e2727dfa6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370463} {:answer/id #uuid "59200cb7-debe-55ab-8fb0-6a5afb506399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "477dd1da-92b7-52aa-a991-99c846c425f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370460} :answer-field/id #uuid "e47ca6b7-d64e-5f09-b330-f2c065bf149e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bfe7ea3-d20b-5a92-b7ac-e6c2ec81d721" :question/math-academy-id "q-40179" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{i})\\ne 0$ for any $i$'s, we conclude that none of them belongs to the kernel of $\\,T.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f6189c-fa3e-5019-b7f9-840275cb3e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370467} {:answer/id #uuid "c0adc9c0-8c90-5eee-bf1c-7d91d4355d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370468} {:answer/id #uuid "587b651e-0212-52f2-b9b2-1b0fb7303bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370469} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c6f8e7-04b2-5f53-9637-7d9f26ce5b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370470} {:answer/id #uuid "8535d721-30bc-5c23-b914-4877120b4ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "587b651e-0212-52f2-b9b2-1b0fb7303bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81405d3-af48-553f-bb05-8f72bc410a96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1375a76-1197-5dc8-9cbc-a9ed71a37739" :question/math-academy-id "q-80723" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the third one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d352ce7e-9e86-5ed2-b3cc-953d4cb61682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370474} {:answer/id #uuid "dc687c50-5415-549c-be66-2e4eb2acf837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370475} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff1f22f-4713-5814-85f3-bc23ccd81044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370476} {:answer/id #uuid "bf86bee8-18fd-5f63-8bc4-9e4d5ff44597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370477} {:answer/id #uuid "70888d7f-08da-53e6-9603-6f48e8e19bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d352ce7e-9e86-5ed2-b3cc-953d4cb61682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370474} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee331383-2503-52f0-865a-c94193793cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fffe6fe8-c932-5eb7-a956-8af5640dc97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370481} {:answer/id #uuid "ef506197-3949-52aa-a82f-9c3143557ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370482} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9af07e-d04c-5f7d-a35c-a1a015bfe54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370483} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0da769-38b2-5770-981c-2c7540747f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370484} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe2ebd-8bfb-5337-aa82-aa6a1eb2a5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe2ebd-8bfb-5337-aa82-aa6a1eb2a5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370485} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ab158c-329a-57de-bb7e-cf6136e033b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28f8f942-a2cb-5643-b1bd-15c5a81a962c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81387" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot k-4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3k+12 \\\\ 2k-8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3k+12=0 \\\\ 2k-8=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21eb175a-5efa-5c5a-bce4-605f003063de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370488} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5994a0-5904-5d01-85f8-530d4316947f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370489} {:answer/id #uuid "370723a8-711d-5a80-9bd3-6db18810cee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370490} {:answer/id #uuid "07f91c3b-b331-5c98-9d95-c7956c20b91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370491} {:answer/id #uuid "fab39608-7666-5ff2-a3ab-12832b114b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21eb175a-5efa-5c5a-bce4-605f003063de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370488} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c23503-794c-51e5-b0e2-c54b8c835076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0da1a486-f77f-5335-92da-99e45355d1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81388" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z \\\\ -6x+3y-3z \\\\ 4x-2y+2z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3-1+k \\\\ -6\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k \\\\ 4\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5+k \\\\ -15-3k \\\\ 10+2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5+k=0 \\\\ -15-3k=0 \\\\ 10+2k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8d0205-fa55-56ea-b853-d548aa774efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370495} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a50f54-acbd-5b80-b354-e912ee942dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370496} {:answer/id #uuid "d875f465-cfd2-5c7f-a16b-3e10dea63e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370497} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d33b5-ee79-527b-8d9c-3266e46600d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370498} {:answer/id #uuid "bddb983c-08ba-551d-99b1-e0a2771b0b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d33b5-ee79-527b-8d9c-3266e46600d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370498} :answer-field/id #uuid "069f5a2d-d6d2-5df1-9f18-2fb82996d311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29b1b7b0-589e-51c0-afb6-47caa8419215" :question/math-academy-id "q-81391" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 16y-6x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3=2\\cdot 1-3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af54e08d-d72d-5101-b7f1-d77e94c2779b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370502} {:answer/id #uuid "bdee2855-8389-5e84-86f8-17fc4b46cef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370503} {:answer/id #uuid "cc05843d-7476-5f8d-a8e5-583921f5c949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370504} {:answer/id #uuid "8294ea1e-4d25-58b2-9e8c-39f820bf6fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370505} {:answer/id #uuid "271e6be6-3260-5be5-9d7e-9e2f3182ba69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc05843d-7476-5f8d-a8e5-583921f5c949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370504} :answer-field/id #uuid "12917101-dcbd-5d98-9e72-3ca7ccf37c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ea31c94-867a-5e75-a463-fbd5411648eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-81392" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$"}]}
